    "texts":[
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Provenance",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tBy 1966, Gillett G. Griffin (1928-2016), Princeton, NJ [1]; 2014, partially sold and partially gifted to the Princeton University Art Museum.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tNotes:\n\t<br />\n\t[1] Griffin lent the work to the Museum in 1966 (L.1966.158).\n\t<br />\n</p>",
            "remarks":null
        },
        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"The relief on this vessel presents a densely packed series of humans with elaborate headdresses, animals, and hieroglyphs in square cartouches with bar-and-dot numerals. These glyphs refer to the 260-day ritual calendar used throughout Mesoamerica, and presumably name the individuals depicted by their dates of birth. The surface decoration was produced by a combination of mold impression with modeling and incision to refine the forms in relief. The use of molds for pottery production seems to have originated at Teotihuacan in the ﬁrst centuries CE, although artists in the Mixtequilla region may have adopted the technology from artists in southern Guatemala, given similarities in subject matter used for mold-made vessels in each region. For example, one side of the bowl depicts a man grasping a deer, likely drawn from the Tequisate style of southern Guatemala.\n",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
